    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We have used a finite element numerical simulation to study fluid flow through fractured porous media. Fractures are represented with an effective porous media model. The magnitude of flow through a single fracture is related to the regional head gradient, the fracture length, the permeability of the fracture, and the fracture orientation with respect to the head gradient. In multiple fracture systems, local perturbations of the head caused by individual fractures interact to change the magnitude of flow and the shape of fluid flow pathways. Discrete fracture models based on outcrop maps from the fractured Aztec Sandstone in the Valley of Fire State Park show a strong correlation between the simulated magnitude of fluid flow and the spatial distribution of chemical alteration on the outcrop.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
